# Groups and directives

Two directive families arrange a document: `//` lines bracket content into
**groups**, and `/cmd()` lines apply one command to the next element. This page
is the grammar; [Commands](commands.sx) is the vocabulary.

## The `//` family

| Line | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `// name cmd()` | opener — a named group |
| `// cmd()` | opener — nameless, when the first token has parens |
| `// --` | separator — start the next section |
| `// end name` | closer — must name the innermost open group |
| `// end` | closer — the innermost group, unnamed |
| `//` | closer — the same, shorter |

// two_col grid(2)

**Left section**

Sections fill left to right. Each one holds as many content elements as you
want — paragraphs, lists, code, anything.

// --

**Right section**

This group is named `two_col`, and the name is optional. The one below opens
with no name at all.

// end two_col

A nameless opener needs parens on its first token, which is how a command is
told apart from a name:

// grid(2)

**Nameless, section one**

Written `// grid(2)` — no name, straight to the command.

// --

**Nameless, section two**

Closed with a bare `//`, which means the same as `// end`.

//

## Groups nest

A group inside a group is ordinary. Different commands nest freely; the same
command does not, and that is the [layout-level rule](#the-layout-level-rule)
below.

// outer grid(2)

// inner_left color(muted)

**A group inside a cell**

This muted group sits entirely inside the left cell of a grid. It is closed
before the separator, so the separator still belongs to `outer`.

// end inner_left

// --

**The right cell**

Separators and closers always bind to the innermost group that is open, so
nesting never needs the author to think about which `//` matches what — only
to close in order.

// end outer

A group with no commands at all is just a named container: it groups content,
creates no layout, and nests as deeply as you like.

## Single commands

A `/command()` line applies one command to the **very next content element**,
with no closer to remember.

/skinny(60%)

This paragraph renders at 60% of the body column, centered in it. Only this
paragraph — the next one is back to full width.

Commands chain: consecutive `/cmd()` lines all bind to the same element,
nesting innermost-last, so a chain is the nested-group form without the
ceremony.

/skinny(70%)

/center()

Narrow *and* centered, from two stacked directives.

## When a directive is not a directive

A `//` line is a directive only if **both** halves of the activation rule hold:
the line parses cleanly, *and* — for separators and closers — a group is
actually open to act on. Failing either one, the line is prose.

The four lines below are all ordinary text on this rendered page. Two are
malformed; two parse perfectly and simply have nothing to close.

// just a comment here

// box glow(5)

// --

// end nothing_is_open

The same holds for `/cmd()` lines: the command must parse, *and* a content
element must follow. A chain that runs into the end of a document, or into
another directive, reverts to prose in full. There is a `/skinny(50%)` line at
the very bottom of this page doing exactly that.

> [!NOTE]
> This is the degradation story in one sentence: strikedown only ever *adds*
> meaning to a line that earns it. [Degradation](degradation.sx) collects the
> near-misses; [Warnings](warnings.sx) covers the lines that do activate and
> still get told something.

## The layout-level rule

Groups nest freely, but **each layout command applies at most once** along any
chain of containers: a `skinny` inside a `grid` cell is fine, a `grid` inside a
`grid` is not. The inner one is ignored with a warning on stderr, and the group
still forms — layout mistakes never break your document's structure.

// nesting_ok grid(2)

/skinny(80%)

A narrow paragraph inside the left cell. Different commands, so this nests
happily.

// --

The right cell.

// end nesting_ok

Only the colliding command is stripped. On an opener carrying several, the rest
still apply — and a group with no layout commands never consumes a level, so a
grid inside a plain named container is fine.

## Unterminated groups

A closer is optional. A group left open runs to the end of the document, which
is what the group below does — there is no `// end` after it, and the page ends
correctly anyway.

// tail_group color(muted)

This muted group is never closed. It swallows the rest of the page, which is
exactly one paragraph and one inert directive line.

/skinny(50%)
